---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { MethodologyAccordion } from "../../components/AnalysisPageSections";
---

<Layout
  role="visitor"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: "Home", href: "/" },
    { label: "Methods", href: "/methods/evaluation-framework" },
    { label: "How the models work" },
  ]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto px-4 sm:px-8 py-12 max-w-4xl">
    <div class="mb-10">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-dark mb-3">How the Models Work</h1>
      <p class="text-gray-600 text-lg">
        The SUM platform uses two analytical methods: Ridge regression for
        impact analysis and PROMETHEE-GAIA for multi-criteria decision analysis.
        This page explains how each model works and how to interpret its
        outputs.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Ridge Regression section -->
    <div class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-dark mb-4 border-b border-gray-200 pb-2">
        Impact Analysis — Ridge Regression
      </h2>
    </div>

    <MethodologyAccordion
      title="Understanding the regression approach"
      subtitle="How policy measure contributions are estimated"
      content=""
      defaultOpen={true}
    >
      <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The impact analysis assesses the effect of push and pull measures —
          combinations of restrictions on private car use (push) and incentives
          for shared mobility adoption (pull) — implemented across multiple
          Living Labs as part of Sustainable Urban Mobility Plan (SUMP)
          interventions.
        </p>
        <p>The analysis follows a three-step process:</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 ml-4">
          <li>
            <strong>Baseline data collection</strong> — KPIs are measured
            before the interventions.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Impact estimation</strong> — KPIs are recalculated after
            implementation and compared to the baseline.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Interactive interface</strong> — Results are communicated
            through a dedicated interface to support stakeholder exploration.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          To quantify the contribution of each measure, a{" "}
          <strong>Ridge regression model</strong> (linear regression with L2
          regularization) is used. Each measure is encoded as a binary variable
          (implemented = 1, not implemented = 0) per Living Lab. The model
          associates a regression coefficient to each measure, estimating its
          individual contribution to the observed change in each KPI. Ridge
          regularization is chosen because the number of measures exceeds the
          number of Living Labs, helping prevent overfitting and address
          multicollinearity.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both univariate (per KPI) and multivariate (grouped KPIs of the same
          type, i.e. environmental, societal, economic) analyses are conducted
          to ensure robustness, especially when data points are limited for
          local KPIs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </MethodologyAccordion>

    <MethodologyAccordion
      title="How to interpret regression results"
      subtitle="Reading regression coefficients and model accuracy"
      content=""
    >
      <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The model outputs a regression coefficient (β) for each measure,
          representing its estimated contribution to the observed change in a
          given KPI:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 ml-4">
          <li>
            A <strong>positive coefficient</strong> indicates the measure
            contributed to an increase in that KPI.
          </li>
          <li>
            A <strong>negative coefficient</strong> indicates a decrease.
          </li>
          <li>
            A coefficient <strong>close to zero</strong> suggests the measure
            had little to no isolated impact on that KPI.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4">
          The <strong>mean squared error (MSE)</strong> of the model provides an
          indication of estimation accuracy. A lower MSE means the model fits
          the observed data more closely.
        </p>
        <p>
          See{" "}
          <a href="/methods/limitations" class="text-primary hover:underline font-medium">
            Limitations
          </a>{" "}
          for important caveats when interpreting these results.
        </p>
      </div>
    </MethodologyAccordion>

    <!-- PROMETHEE-GAIA section -->
    <div class="mb-8 mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-dark mb-4 border-b border-gray-200 pb-2">
        MCDA — PROMETHEE and GAIA
      </h2>
    </div>

    <MethodologyAccordion
      title="What is PROMETHEE?"
      subtitle="Multi-criteria decision analysis method"
      content=""
      defaultOpen={true}
    >
      <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          PROMETHEE (Preference Ranking Organisation METHod for Enrichment
          Evaluations) is a Multi-Criteria Decision Analysis (MCDA) method used
          to evaluate and rank alternatives when multiple, and sometimes
          conflicting, criteria must be considered simultaneously.
        </p>
        <p>
          It handles both quantitative data (e.g. costs, travel times) and
          qualitative factors (e.g. environmental impact, social acceptability),
          making it well-suited for complex decisions like urban transport policy
          evaluation. It also incorporates the diverse perspectives of multiple
          stakeholders (citizens, businesses, public authorities) by building
          preference relations between alternatives based on how each group
          values the criteria.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the SUM platform, KPI groups serve as PROMETHEE criteria, and
          policy measures or business activities serve as alternatives. The
          three pre-defined perspectives (Regulatory Authorities, Public
          Transport Operators, New Shared Mobility Providers) reflect different
          stakeholder weightings of these criteria.
        </p>
      </div>
    </MethodologyAccordion>

    <MethodologyAccordion
      title="What is GAIA?"
      subtitle="Geometric Analysis for Interactive Aid"
      content=""
    >
      <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          GAIA (Geometrical Analysis for Interactive Aid) is the visual,
          interactive companion to PROMETHEE. It displays evaluation results
          graphically on a GAIA plane, mapping both the policy alternatives and
          the evaluation criteria together.
        </p>
        <p>
          This visual representation helps stakeholders (regardless of their
          technical background) better understand the analysis, communicate
          results, and identify trade-offs and areas where preferences converge
          or conflict.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 ml-4">
          <li>
            <strong>Criteria pointing in the same direction</strong> indicate
            aligned objectives.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Criteria pointing in opposite directions</strong> reveal
            trade-offs.
          </li>
          <li>
            The <strong>decision stick</strong> summarises the overall
            PROMETHEE ranking direction.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>GAIA quality</strong> (0–100%) indicates how well the 2D
            projection preserves the full decision information.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </MethodologyAccordion>

    <MethodologyAccordion
      title="How to interpret PROMETHEE-GAIA results"
      subtitle="Reading rankings, net flows, and the GAIA plane"
      content=""
    >
      <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          PROMETHEE-GAIA results combine two complementary outputs:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 ml-4">
          <li>
            <strong>PROMETHEE ranking</strong> — alternatives ordered by net
            flow (φ). Higher φ means the alternative outperforms others across
            more criteria.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>GAIA plane</strong> — visual mapping of alternatives and
            criteria onto 2D. Policies positioned close to a criterion's axis
            perform well on that specific criterion.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Together, they allow users to rank alternatives, identify trade-offs,
          and explore consensus areas across stakeholder perspectives.
        </p>
        <p>
          See{" "}
          <a href="/methods/limitations" class="text-primary hover:underline font-medium">
            Limitations
          </a>{" "}
          for important caveats.
        </p>
      </div>
    </MethodologyAccordion>
  </div>
</Layout>
